  "step_2": "The individual's verbal report states: '1% wird wahrscheinlich nie eintreffen, also macht man bei B nur Gewinn' (1% is unlikely to occur, so in B one only gets profit). This indicates that the individual disregards the 1% chance of 0 Euros as negligible and focuses on the fact that in Lottery B, the remaining 99% of the time, the outcome is a positive amount (2500 or 2400 Euros). The individual concludes that B only yields profit, implying a preference for B due to the certainty of positive outcomes. However, the report does not mention skewness, probability distribution shape, or any comparison of how much probability is assigned to favorable versus unfavorable outcomes. Instead, the reasoning centers on the absence of loss (no negative outcome) and the perception that B only yields profit. There is no reference to the concentration of probability on higher values or the concept of left-skewness. The individual does not discuss the distribution of outcomes or their relative positions in terms of skewness. The justification is based on the perceived certainty of profit, not on the statistical skewness of the lottery distributions.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — the skewness of the lotteries — is not considered by the individual. The individual ignores the probabilistic structure of the outcomes and focuses only on the absence of loss (the 1% zero outcome being negligible). The individual does not compare the distribution of probabilities across favorable and unfavorable outcomes, nor does the individual mention or evaluate the concept of left-skewness. The decision reason requires recognizing that B has a greater portion of probability mass on favorable outcomes (2500 and 2400) compared to unfavorable ones (0), which makes it more left-skewed. This is not present in the report. The individual’s justification is based on a different reasoning — the absence of loss — which is not equivalent to evaluating skewness. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual did not apply the rule that prefers the more left-skewed lottery. Instead, they used a different heuristic (profit-only outcome).",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
